#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
code-intel-dash — local health dashboard for the code-intelligence stack.

    code-intel-dash                 serve on http://127.0.0.1:7717
    code-intel-dash --port 8080     somewhere else
    code-intel-dash --once          print one JSON report and exit
    code-intel-dash --open          serve and open a browser

WHY THIS DOES NOT PROBE ANYTHING ITSELF
    Every question about workspaces, mappings, watchers and config is answered
    by `agent-code-intel --status --all --json`, never re-implemented here. A
    second implementation of "is the watcher running" drifts from the first one
    silently, and then BOTH keep printing green. agent-code-intel's own header
    makes the same point about its internal probes; this is that rule crossing
    a process boundary.

    What this file adds is the part agent-code-intel deliberately has no opinion
    about: live functional checks (can ollama actually embed? does a search
    actually return rows?), stored statistics (qdrant point counts, GitNexus
    graph size), and watcher logs. Those are monitoring concerns, not
    reconciler concerns.

LIVENESS IS NOT CORRECTNESS
    "The process is up" is the weak claim. Each component therefore gets a
    functional check that exercises the thing it is actually for:

        ollama      embed a string, count the dimensions, time it
        qdrant      read collection stats; a green collection with zero
                    points is a broken index, not a healthy one
        GrepAI      run a real query against a real workspace
        GitNexus    read .gitnexus/meta.json: node/edge counts, indexed
                    commit vs HEAD, and the Node version the index was
                    BUILT with — an index built under a Node without
                    module.registerHooks is the trap that costs an afternoon
        watcher     tail its log; a running watcher with no log activity
                    since a code change is the silent-staleness failure

BINDS TO LOOPBACK ONLY
    The report names filesystem paths, container state and project layout.
    127.0.0.1, no auth, not meant to leave the machine.

No third-party dependencies: standard library only, same as the rest of the
stack assumes.
"""

